     eemmuullaattee [ --llLLRR ] [ {zzsshh|sshh|kksshh|ccsshh} [ _f_l_a_g_s ... ] ]
            Without any argument print current emulation mode.

            With single argument set up zsh options to emulate the specified
            shell as much as possible.  ccsshh will never be fully emulated.  If
            the argument is not one of the shells listed above, zzsshh will be
            used as a default; more precisely, the tests performed on the
            argument are the same as those used to determine the emulation at
            startup based on the shell name, see the section COMPATIBILITY in
            _z_s_h(1) .  In addition to setting shell options, the command also
            restores the pristine state of pattern enables, as if all patterns
            had been enabled using eennaabbllee --pp.

            If the eemmuullaattee command occurs inside a function that has been
            marked for execution tracing with ffuunnccttiioonnss --tt then the xxttrraaccee
            option will be turned on regardless of emulation mode or other
            options.  Note that code executed inside the function by the ..,
            ssoouurrccee, or eevvaall commands is not considered to be running directly
            from the function, hence does not provoke this behaviour.

            If the --RR switch is given, all settable options are reset to their
            default value corresponding to the specified emulation mode,
            except for certain options describing the interactive environment;
            otherwise, only those options likely to cause portability problems
            in scripts and functions are altered.  If the --LL switch is given,
            the options LLOOCCAALL__OOPPTTIIOONNSS, LLOOCCAALL__PPAATTTTEERRNNSS and LLOOCCAALL__TTRRAAPPSS will be
            set as well, causing the effects of the eemmuullaattee command and any
            sseettoopptt, ddiissaabbllee --pp or eennaabbllee --pp, and ttrraapp commands to be local to
            the immediately surrounding shell function, if any; normally these
            options are turned off in all emulation modes except kksshh. The --LL
            switch is mutually exclusive with the use of --cc in _f_l_a_g_s.

            If there is a single argument and the --ll switch is given, the
            options that would be set or unset (the latter indicated with the
            prefix `nnoo') are listed.  --ll can be combined with --LL or --RR and the
            list will be modified in the appropriate way.  Note the list does
            not depend on the current setting of options, i.e. it includes all
            options that may in principle change, not just those that would
            actually change.

            The _f_l_a_g_s may be any of the invocation-time flags described in the
            section INVOCATION in _z_s_h(1), except that `--oo EEMMAACCSS' and `--oo VVII'
            may not be used.  Flags such as `++rr'/`++oo RREESSTTRRIICCTTEEDD' may be
            prohibited in some circumstances.

            If --cc _a_r_g appears in _f_l_a_g_s, _a_r_g is evaluated while the requested
            emulation is temporarily in effect.  In this case the emulation
            mode and all options are restored to their previous values before
            eemmuullaattee returns.  The --RR switch may precede the name of the shell
            to emulate; note this has a meaning distinct from including --RR in
            _f_l_a_g_s.

            Use of --cc enables `sticky' emulation mode for functions defined
            within the evaluated expression:  the emulation mode is associated
            thereafter with the function so that whenever the function is
            executed the emulation (respecting the --RR switch, if present) and
            all options are set (and pattern disables cleared) before entry to
            the function, and the state is restored after exit.  If the
            function is called when the sticky emulation is already in effect,
            either within an `eemmuullaattee _s_h_e_l_l --cc' expression or within another
            function with the same sticky emulation, entry and exit from the
            function do not cause options to be altered (except due to
            standard processing such as the LLOOCCAALL__OOPPTTIIOONNSS option).  This also
            applies to functions marked for autoload within the sticky
            emulation; the appropriate set of options will be applied at the
            point the function is loaded as well as when it is run.

            For example:

                   eemmuullaattee sshh --cc ''ffnnii(()) {{ sseettoopptt ccsshhnnuullllgglloobb;; }}
                   ffnnoo(()) {{ ffnnii;; }}''
                   ffnnoo

            The two functions ffnnii and ffnnoo are defined with sticky sshh
            emulation.  ffnnoo is then executed, causing options associated with
            emulations to be set to their values in sshh.  ffnnoo then calls ffnnii;
            because ffnnii is also marked for sticky sshh emulation, no option
            changes take place on entry to or exit from it.  Hence the option
            ccsshhnnuullllgglloobb, turned off by sshh emulation, will be turned on within
            ffnnii and remain on return to ffnnoo.  On exit from ffnnoo, the emulation
            mode and all options will be restored to the state they were in
            before entry to the temporary emulation.

            The documentation above is typically sufficient for the intended
            purpose of executing code designed for other shells in a suitable
            environment.  More detailed rules follow.
            1.     The sticky emulation environment provided by `eemmuullaattee _s_h_e_l_l
                   --cc' is identical to that provided by entry to a function
                   marked for sticky emulation as a consequence of being
                   defined in such an environment.  Hence, for example, the
                   sticky emulation is inherited by subfunctions defined
                   within functions with sticky emulation.
            2.     No change of options takes place on entry to or exit from
                   functions that are not marked for sticky emulation, other
                   than those that would normally take place, even if those
                   functions are called within sticky emulation.
            3.     No special handling is provided for functions marked for
                   aauuttoollooaadd nor for functions present in wordcode created by
                   the zzccoommppiillee command.
            4.     The presence or absence of the --RR switch to eemmuullaattee
                   corresponds to different sticky emulation modes, so for
                   example `eemmuullaattee sshh --cc', `eemmuullaattee --RR sshh --cc' and `eemmuullaattee
                   ccsshh --cc' are treated as three distinct sticky emulations.
            5.     Difference in shell options supplied in addition to the
                   basic emulation also mean the sticky emulations are
                   different, so for example `eemmuullaattee zzsshh --cc' and `eemmuullaattee zzsshh
                   --oo ccbbaasseess --cc' are treated as distinct sticky emulations.

