                   may be created this way, but it may be assigned to in the
                   ttyyppeesseett statement only if the reserved word form of ttyyppeesseett
                   is enabled (as it is by default).  When displaying, both
                   normal and associative arrays are shown.

            --ff     The names refer to functions rather than parameters.  No
                   assignments can be made, and the only other valid flags are
                   --tt, --TT, --kk, --uu, --UU and --zz.  The flag --tt turns on execution
                   tracing for this function; the flag --TT does the same, but
                   turns off tracing for any named (not anonymous) function
                   called from the present one, unless that function also has
                   the --tt or --TT flag.  The --uu and --UU flags cause the function
                   to be marked for autoloading; --UU also causes alias
                   expansion to be suppressed when the function is loaded.
                   See the description of the `aauuttoollooaadd' builtin for details.

                   Note that the builtin ffuunnccttiioonnss provides the same basic
                   capabilities as ttyyppeesseett --ff but gives access to a few extra
                   options; aauuttoollooaadd gives further additional options for the
                   case ttyyppeesseett --ffuu and ttyyppeesseett --ffUU.

